00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:17.400
In 2017, over 20 million people visited the
most popular theme park in the world: Magic

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.550
Kingdom at Walt Disney World in Florida.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:27.039
Together, Disney parks, including Disneyland
Anaheim, Tokyo, Paris, and Hong Kong, attract

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:31.730
150 million annual visitors; more than any
other.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:39.940
One could argue, however, and I will, there’s
one even more popular: IKEA, with 460 million.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.320
Let me explain.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.500
We in the biz know there are many definitions
of ‘theme park’ - the academic definition,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.950
my definition, and, when you’re ready to
really dive deep in the literature, you turn

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:57.170
to the experts: the International Association
of Amusement Parks and Attractions, who define

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.760
“theme park” as: “an amusement park
that has themed attractions, be it food, costumes,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.810
entertainment, retail stores and/or rides”.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:09.750
Check, close enough, check, check, and check.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:15.180
Shopping at IKEA is a full-day activity - you
hop in the family van, drive outside the city,

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.630
drop your kids in the play area, max out your
credit cards, and then top it off with some

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.860
Swedish meatballs.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.350
The IKEA brand is as iconic and loved as Disney,
Six Flags, and Universal.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:33.590
The only slight problem is with the ’s’
in “rides” - IKEA has just one: the unidirectional

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:38.120
pathway which guides you through the store,
into the warehouse, and the restaurant.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.630
The ride might be called “Käpitalism”.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.689
The theme of the park, Sweden.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:49.659
Although, despite its colors, names, and advertised
philosophy, IKEA is not, strictly speaking,

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:50.939
actually Swedish.

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:54.509
Or, technically, a company at all.

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:58.580
What are the secrets behind IKEA’s enormous
global success?

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.710
Although not nearly the same company it is
today, Ingvar Kamprad founded IKEA in 1943

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.760
as a traveling salesman, selling staples like
matches, pencils, and watches.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.800
Seven years later, he began selling furniture.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:21.090
Seeing the logistical difficulty of shipping
large items far away, Ingvar decided to flat-pack

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.620
the furniture, which also reduced the chance
of damage during transport.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:31.120
The company created a catalog that customers
could browse through and order with from afar.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.720
The first showroom was built in 1953 in the
same small town, followed by the first of

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.290
the company’s iconic giant warehouses in
1965.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.430
The business model has more or less remained
consistent ever since.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.459
Rather than try to compete with department
stores in city centers, IKEA has always preferred

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:55.560
large multi-story buildings away from the
bustle, with only a few exceptions like in

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.360
cramped Hong Kong.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.940
With this far cheaper real estate, the company
can build equally giant attached parking lots.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:09.800
The average size of a store is around 35,000
square meters or 370,000 square feet, with

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.290
over 8,000 unique items.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:16.480
Inside, the experience is unlike any other.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.340
Customers are guided through the store by
a one-way path, which begins in the showroom,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.959
flows into the “Market Hall” where small
items can be picked up instantly, and ends

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:30.400
in the warehouse, where previously selected
items can be picked off the shelves and then

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.989
paid for at the register.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:38.209
Most retailers design their stores to maximize
the time you spend inside, spreading out staples

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:40.570
like bread, milk, and bananas.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.900
IKEA takes this approach to the extreme.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.430
By arranging its stores like a transit line,
the company has total control over the shopping

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:52.700
experience and forces you to look through
all its departments.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.030
Placed toward the beginning of the journey
are usually its largest drivers of profit:

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.810
bedroom and kitchen.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:04.299
The restaurant and kids’ play area not only
keep customers inside longer but also communicate

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:09.220
low prices and value, which spill over onto
the brand, as a whole.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.630
The average visit lasts a whole hour and a
half at many of its stores, and, by one estimate,

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.209
60% of items purchased at IKEA are unplanned.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:21.750
Nudged towards, in other words, by the store
psychology.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:27.030
Research also suggests we attach more value
to things we help make ourselves - turning

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:30.530
IKEA’s self-assembly from a flaw to a feature.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:37.040
It also helps reduce manufacturing and shipping
costs - another ingredient in the IKEA formula.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.620
Low prices are its self-proclaimed mission
- to, quote, “Democratize furniture”.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:48.070
Whatever its true motivation, IKEA has remained
inexpensive despite its ubiquity.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:53.479
The price of its most popular chair, for example,
has gone down dramatically over time, adjusted

00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:54.970
for inflation.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.270
The company reportedly sets a target price
for a product before sending it off to its

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.670
small team of around 20 designers, who work
within those constraints.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:08.830
Finally, the warehouse design eliminates the
need for a large staff, which greatly reduces

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.850
operating expenses.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:16.539
This low-cost image is so important that,
before he died in 2018, the founder and CEO

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:22.039
claimed only to fly economy, never stay in
luxury hotels, and use public transit - staying

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:24.250
true to the company’s values.

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:29.940
But, perhaps even more important than its
psychology, warehouse design, or low prices

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.880
is IKEA’s impenetrable brand.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.680
As it began expanding internationally, first
in Switzerland in 1973, then throughout Europe

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:43.039
for the next few decades, it greatly emphasized
its Swedishness.

00:05:43.039 --> 00:05:50.360
From 2000 until 2009, the company opened 150
stores, more than it had in the past 50 years,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.620
many in North America and Asia.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:59.610
Today, with over 200,000 employees and 433
stores, IKEA is not only the largest retailer

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:04.819
of furniture but also the world’s largest
consumer of wood, period.

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:09.100
Its strategy has been to enter many different
markets, each with only a small number of

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.080
stores at the most optimal locations.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.020
The US, for example, is its second-largest
market by number of stores, despite having

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:22.250
only 50, and 17 countries or territories have
only 1.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:26.449
The success of this approach is proof of IKEA’s
global brand.

00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:31.330
While many companies enter a new market by
localizing their message and product, IKEA

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:36.610
has succeeded by doing just the opposite:
embracing its Swedish origins.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:42.330
The IKEA image is so strong around the world,
that it can open a single store in an entire

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.009
country and still be instantly recognized
and loved.

00:06:46.009 --> 00:06:50.550
From the minimal, functional design of its
furniture, to their famously hard to pronounce

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:54.449
names, IKEA is unmistakably Swedish.

00:06:54.449 --> 00:06:59.020
Its bright blue and yellow stores announce
its heritage from afar.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:04.100
Beyond its visual design, IKEA also exports
Scandinavian egalitarianism.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:09.450
Employees, at all levels, are encouraged to
address one another by first names, and not

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.599
titles.

00:07:10.599 --> 00:07:15.190
Corporate executives are asked to participate
in so-called “Anti-bureaucratic weeks”

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.510
where they work on store floors to experience
daily operations and empathize with their

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:20.789
colleagues.

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:25.020
Not only is this association with its home
country good for the company, but it’s also

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:31.320
good for Sweden - who has encouraged and greatly
benefitted from IKEA’s positive reputation.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.819
Swedish embassies, for example, actively participate
in new store openings and have even sponsored

00:07:36.819 --> 00:07:37.979
them financially.

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:44.270
Ironically, despite all this, IKEA is, legally,
more Dutch than Swedish.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:49.479
The company is actually owned by a Dutch Holding
company whose profits end up in a tax-exempt

00:07:49.479 --> 00:07:55.410
non-profit, also Dutch, foundation, who indirectly
channels the money through another Dutch foundation

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.009
with nearly the same name.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:02.729
It’s intellectual property, meanwhile, is
owned by a holding company called Inter IKEA,

00:08:02.729 --> 00:08:07.190
this time, registered in Luxembourg, which,
in turn, is owned by a company of the exact

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:13.129
same name in the Netherlands Antilles, who,
yes, is also owned by a trust company.

00:08:13.129 --> 00:08:15.770
And that’s a simplified explanation.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:21.729
All this legal fine print makes IKEA the 3rd
largest charity in the world, only 30% smaller

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:24.259
than the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation.

00:08:24.259 --> 00:08:29.500
IKEA is certainly not unique in its complicated
legal structure, but, in registering as a

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:34.719
charitable foundation, it goes beyond the
norms of most global companies.

00:08:34.719 --> 00:08:39.539
All this, it claims, is designed to protect
control of the company from outside parties,

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:42.820
including, in fairness, the founder’s own
family.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:48.130
Critics, though, argue control is only a secondary
benefit after tax avoidance.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:55.620
Legally, physically, and financially, IKEA
is, today, more global than Swedish.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:00.660
And yet it has one of the strongest, most
resilient brands in history.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:06.220
IKEA has come to embody a certain, rare kind
of friendly-capitalism.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:11.370
After all, despite its size and controversies,
who could hate a theme park?

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:16.750
In its embrace of capitalism and likeness
to an amusement park, IKEA is slightly reminiscent

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:22.310
of another unique place on earth I’m making
a documentary about - coming soon to Nebula,

